# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): each line researched against written sources and Wikidata,
# re-checked independently, the titles taken from Wikidata by script, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-18T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://www.wikipedia.org/> ,
              <https://en.wikiquote.org/> ,
              <https://fr.wikiquote.org/wiki/La_Haine> ,
              <https://imsdb.com/> ,
              <https://www.springfieldspringfield.co.uk/> ,
              <https://www.moviemistakes.com/> ,
              <https://www.citation-celebre.com/citations/82615> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "Selection: the compiler's own choice of 119 candidate lines that are widely known on their own and each name one film, not a published ranking. The AFI's 100 Years…100 Movie Quotes list, through its English Wikipedia article, was used to check wording and speakers, not as the selection: 44 of the 95 lines are also on that list of 100, without its ranking, and the others are not, many from films it could not include (released from 2004 on, after its cutoff, or not American). A candidate was left out when its wording could not be confirmed in two written sources, or the form usually quoted is not what is said and the sources disagree on what is; when the line on the card is also said in another film of the same story (a sequel, a prequel, a remake, another film of the franchise or another film of the same play), or such a film could not be checked for it; a line that an unrelated film quotes in homage is kept (White Heat's in Ricochet, On the Waterfront's in Raging Bull); when the line is a song lyric or too common to name a film; or when the line as the sources record it differs by a word from the form usually quoted. One line per film: where a film had two candidates, the second was left out. 95 cards remain, 94 in English and one in French (La Haine)."@en ,
                 "1. Wording: for each candidate the line was looked up on the film's English Wikiquote page through the MediaWiki API (the French Wikiquote for La Haine, with citation-celebre.com), and confirmed in at least one more written source: the film's English Wikipedia article or the Wikipedia article on the AFI list, a published screenplay on IMSDb, a subtitle transcript on Springfield! Springfield!, or Movie Mistakes. Three lines are not on their film's Wikiquote page and rest on two other written sources instead: Die Hard (the Springfield! Springfield! transcript and Movie Mistakes), The Wolf of Wall Street (the IMSDb screenplay and the transcript) and Se7en (the English Wikipedia article and the transcript). The lines were checked against these written sources, not against the films themselves. Where the sources differ in punctuation, the card follows the majority (\"Well, nobody's perfect!\", \"No. I am your father.\"); where the second source differs only by a filler word or a contraction, the card follows Wikiquote (\"life, uh... finds a way\", \"Why'd it have to be snakes?\"); where the famous form is a misquote, the card has what the sources record (\"Toto, I've a feeling we're not in Kansas anymore\", \"La-dee-da, la-dee-da\"). A line was shortened by leaving out whole sentences before or after it; two long passages are also cut at a clause and the cut marked with an ellipsis, to quote no more than names the film: Braveheart, from \"tell our enemies\", and La Haine, its first sentence and its last words. Straight quotation marks and apostrophes are used throughout. Pages consulted only for candidates that were left out are not listed among the sources, since nothing on the cards rests on them."@en ,
                 "2. Speaker and film: the character and actor were taken from Wikidata's cast statements (P161 with the P453 character role qualifier), or from the film's Wikipedia or Wikiquote cast list, with the name the scene and the credits use (Maximus, not Wikidata's Maximus Decimus Meridius; Verbal Kint, who says the line, not his other identity Keyser Söze); a voiced character names its voice actor. The film's Wikidata item was checked to be a film (P31), and the year on the note is the year of its earliest publication date (P577)."@en ,
                 "3. Titles: a script fetched each film's Wikidata labels in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (the multilingual label where a language has none), its Wikipedia sitelinks in those languages, P31 and P577, and compared the year with the research. The back is the Wikidata label in each language, with these departures: where the label differs from the title of the film's Wikipedia article in that language only in punctuation or capitalization, the article's title is used (Terminator 2 – Domedagen, Aliens – Återkomsten and År 2001 – ett rymdäventyr in Swedish, Harry el Sucio in Spanish, When Harry Met Sally... and Monty Python's Life of Brian in English); Titanic has no Spanish label, so the Spanish article's title is used; Star Wars and The Empire Strikes Back are given in every language under the titles of their first releases, as the Swedish, German and Korean labels have them, not the later episode titles (in English the titles of the English articles; in Spanish La guerra de las galaxias and El Imperio contraataca, and in French L'Empire contre-attaque, the first-release titles the Spanish and French articles give); and the article's title is used where the label is the original or an older title rather than the one readers know the film by (in Swedish Citizen Kane, not the 1940s release title En sensation, and Social Network; in Spanish El padrino II, the label being the English title, and Independence Day, the label lacking its accent, \"Dia de la independencia\"; in French Le Parrain, 2e partie, not Le Parrain 2; in Korean 몬티 파이튼과 성배, the standard spelling of the article, not 파이톤)."@en ,
                 "4. Notes: \"<character> (<actor>), <year>\", in each language; the character's name as credited, in Latin script. Four notes are worded in each language: The Empire Strikes Back (Darth Vader, played by David Prowse and voiced by James Earl Jones), Field of Dreams (the voice is credited only as \"Himself\" and its actor is unconfirmed), Top Gun (said by two characters) and When Harry Met Sally... (the customer is credited as \"Older Woman Customer\"). The fronts' and backs' labels and the notes are the compiler's own wording."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck's own work, the selection, the titles, years and casts, the labels and the notes, is released under CC0 1.0 like the rest of the library. Its content sources are Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0, and, for some titles and casts, facts from Wikipedia and Wikiquote: a film's title, used to name the film, and who plays whom are not anyone's protected expression. The lines on the fronts are short quotations from the films, which may be protected by their films' copyright, quoted to identify each film, which the back names; they are not covered by CC0, and REUSE.toml says so beside the deck's license. The lines are given as the sources record the films' dialogue; nothing of the sources' own, such as commentary, selection, transcription notes or layout, was copied. Wikiquote and Wikipedia (CC BY-SA), IMSDb, Springfield! Springfield!, Movie Mistakes and citation-celebre.com were used to verify wording, speakers and films."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Research: each candidate's wording, speaker, film and year, and whether the line is also said in another film"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: all 119 candidates, in ten batches, each against Wikiquote, a second written source, Wikidata and the Wikiquote pages of sequels and remakes; six candidates whose pages could not be fetched at first (rate limits) were researched again in a second pass, and for five kept candidates (Dead Poets Society, The Shining, Citizen Kane, Some Like It Hot, Wall Street) the Wikidata item and year could not be fetched and were checked in round 1."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 19 candidates were left out for the reasons under Selection (two more in round 1, one when the deck was built and one in round 3; White Heat, first left out, was brought back in round 4), besides two second lines for films that already had one; for the rest the wording was corrected to the sources where the proposal differed (for example \"No. I am your father.\", \"I'm simply saying that life, uh... finds a way.\", the whole sentence of the Braveheart speech, and the speaker of The Notebook's line, Noah, not Allie)."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Independent re-check: wording, other films, Wikidata item and year, speaker"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every line kept in round 0, re-checked from fresh fetches, with the research's sources shown but not relied on: the wording against Wikiquote and one other written source, a search for the line in other films, the Wikidata item and year, and the character and actor."@en ,
                 "Outcome: two lines were left out (one is on neither its film's Wikiquote page nor its screenplay, the other is from a play filmed again later); some wordings were corrected (\"Well, nobody's perfect!\", the Truman Show line from \"In case\"), and the Field of Dreams voice was recorded as credited only as \"Himself\", its actor unconfirmed."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks: titles, years and film items against Wikidata; the release against the library's shapes and DCAT-AP"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every card's Wikidata item (P31 film, earliest P577 year, labels and Wikipedia sitelinks in the six languages), and the built release with npm run library and npm run library:check (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: every item is a film and every year agrees with the research; the departures from the Wikidata labels are listed under step 3. The release validates."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final review of the built release: language, cards, provenance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the title, description, keywords, labels and worded notes in all six languages; every card's front, backs and note against the research record and the Wikidata labels and sitelinks, doubtful titles against each language's Wikipedia; every claim of the provenance against the research and re-check results and the sources' pages."@en ,
                 "Outcome: fixed: one Swedish verb in the description; eight backs (the first-release titles of Star Wars and The Empire Strikes Back in Spanish and French, Citizen Kane and Social Network in Swedish, The Godfather Part II in Spanish and French, the Korean spelling of Monty Python); one card left out as too common; the description and steps 1 and 4 made exact (three lines not on Wikiquote, where casts come from, which other films count, the Field of Dreams credit); sources that decided wordings added to the record; and the scope of rounds 0 and 1 stated as it was."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing review: quotation, the sources' licenses and terms, copying, the record against what was done"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck's license and its exception for the quoted lines; quotation under US fair use, the EU quotation exception (Directive 2001/29/EC art. 5(3)(d)), Swedish and Korean copyright law; every source's license and terms of use; the deck's text against Wikiquote, Wikipedia and the AFI list for copying, and the overlap with the AFI list; every claim of the provenance against the research record."@en ,
                 "Outcome: no source's license is breached: nothing of the sources' own expression is in the deck. Fixed: Braveheart's and La Haine's fronts cut to what names the film; REUSE.toml states that CC0 does not cover the quoted lines; the sources kept to those whose terms allow this use, each card resting on two of them (Springfield! Springfield!'s terms make its transcripts available for research including text and data mining; Movie Mistakes asks to be credited as a source); White Heat brought back, re-checked from Wikiquote, Wikipedia and the Springfield! Springfield! transcript, since the rule counts only films of the same story; and the record of casts, sources, the AFI overlap and the selection made exact."@en .

